BoilerplatePattern.recommendation:
BOILERPLATE DETECTED: 104 implementations of Flag trait (100% method uniformity).

This file contains repetitive trait implementations that should be 
macro-ified or code-generated.

RECOMMENDED APPROACH:
1. Create a declarative macro to generate Flag implementations
2. Replace 104 trait impl blocks with macro invocations
3. Expected reduction: 7800 lines → ~832 lines (89% reduction)

SHARED METHODS ACROSS IMPLEMENTATIONS:
update, doc_short, name_long, doc_category, is_switch, and 1 more

EXAMPLE TRANSFORMATION:

// Before: ~75 lines per implementation
struct MyFlag;
impl Flag for MyFlag {
fn name_long(&self) -> &'static str { "my-flag" }
fn is_switch(&self) -> bool { true }
fn doc_category(&self) -> &'static str { "general" }
// ... 70 more boilerplate lines
}

// After: ~8 lines per implementation
flag! {
MyFlag {
long: "my-flag",
is_switch: true,
category: "general",
// ... declarative config
}
}

ALTERNATIVES:
- Build-time code generation from schema file (JSON/TOML)
- Use existing derive macro crates if applicable
- Consider procedural macros for complex transformations

CONFIDENCE: 100%

This is NOT a god object requiring module splitting. The high method/line count
is due to declarative boilerplate, not complexity. Focus on reducing repetition
through macros rather than splitting into multiple files.

